<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>heredity-and-evolution</title></head><body><h1>heredity-and-evolution</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Mendel's laws of inheritance</li><li>Incomplete dominance and codominance</li></ul><h2>Mendel's laws of inheritance</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Gregor Mendel - Father of Genetics</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Mendel conducted experiments on pea plants (Pisum sativum) from 1856-1863. He studied 7 pairs of contrasting characters.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Key Terms</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Gene**: Unit of heredity, determines a trait</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **Allele**: Alternative form of a gene</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Dominant**: Expressed when present (capital letter)</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>- **Recessive**: Expressed only when homozygous (lowercase)</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>- **Genotype**: Genetic makeup (e.g., TT, Tt, tt)</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Phenotype**: Physical appearance</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- **Homozygous**: Same alleles (TT or tt)</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **Heterozygous**: Different alleles (Tt)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- **Monohybrid cross**: Cross involving one trait</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>- **Dihybrid cross**: Cross involving two traits</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>### 3. Mendel's First Law - Law of Segregation</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>During gamete formation, the two alleles for each gene separate (segregate) so that each gamete receives only one allele.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Monohybrid cross ratio: Genotypic 1:2:1, Phenotypic 3:1</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>### 4. Mendel's Second Law - Law of Independent Assortment</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Genes for different traits assort independently during gamete formation (applies to genes on different chromosomes).</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Dihybrid cross ratio: Phenotypic 9:3:3:1</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>### 5. Test Cross</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Cross between dominant phenotype and homozygous recessive to determine genotype.</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>### 6. Entrance Exam Focus</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Solve Punnett squares; Calculate ratios; Distinguish between laws.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Monohybrid cross Tt x Tt: F2 genotypes 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, phenotypes 3 tall : 1 short.</td></tr><tr><td>Dihybrid cross TtRr x TtRr: F2 phenotypes 9 : 3 : 3 : 1 (9 round-yellow, 3 round-green, 3 wrinkled-yellow, 1 wrinkled-green).</td></tr><tr><td>A Tt x Tt parent forms T and t gametes in equal numbers, giving a 1:1 ratio of the two gamete types.</td></tr><tr><td>A test cross ratio of 1:1 identifies the unknown dominant parent as heterozygous.</td></tr><tr><td>Genotypic and phenotypic ratios differ only where dominance makes two different genotypes look identical (TT and Tt look the same).</td></tr><tr><td>Sutton and Boveri later supplied the chromosomal basis for Mendel's factors, establishing that genes are carried on chromosomes.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>x Dominant traits are more common. x Dominant just means expressed when present.</td></tr><tr><td>x Independent assortment applies to all genes. x Only applies to genes on different chromosomes.</td></tr><tr><td>x 3:1 ratio always means homozygous dominant. x Could be heterozygous.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gregor Mendel worked on the garden pea Pisum sativum from 1856 to 1863 with seven pairs of contrasting characters and formulated the two laws of classical inheritance. The law of segregation states that the two alleles of a gene separate during gamete formation so that each gamete receives one allele, which gives an F2 genotypic ratio of 1:2:1 and a phenotypic ratio of 3:1 in a monohybrid cross. The law of independent assortment states that genes on different chromosomes assort independently during meiosis, giving the 9:3:3:1 F2 phenotypic ratio in a dihybrid cross. Punnett squares, ratio identification and test crosses are the calculation skills tested in NEB entrance papers.</p><hr><h2>Incomplete dominance and codominance</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Beyond Mendel - Non-Mendelian Inheritance</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Not all traits follow simple dominant-recessive patterns.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>### 2. Incomplete Dominance</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- Neither allele is completely dominant</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- Heterozygote shows intermediate phenotype</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- Example: Snapdragon flowers - Red (RR) x White (WW) -> Pink (RW)</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>- Phenotypic ratio matches genotypic ratio: 1:2:1</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>### 3. Codominance</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- Both alleles are fully expressed in heterozygote</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- Neither masks the other</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- Example: ABO blood groups - IA and IB are codominant</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- Example: Roan cattle - red and white hairs both appear</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>### 4. Multiple Alleles</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>- More than two alleles exist for a gene in the population</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>- Individual still has only two alleles</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>- Example: ABO blood groups - IA, IB, i (three alleles)</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>### 5. Blood Group Inheritance</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>- IAIA or IAi = Group A</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>- IBIB or IBi = Group B</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>- IAIB = Group AB (codominance)</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>- ii = Group O (recessive)</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>- Anti-A antibodies in type B and O; Anti-B antibodies in type A and O</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>### 6. Entrance Exam Focus</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Distinguish incomplete dominance vs codominance; Solve blood group crosses.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Red x white snapdragon: the F1 are all pink, and pink x pink gives F2 of 1 red : 2 pink : 1 white.</td></tr><tr><td>Whenever the heterozygote can be told apart, the phenotypic ratio equals the genotypic ratio of 1:2:1.</td></tr><tr><td>Complete dominance gives a 1:2:1 genotypic ratio but a 3:1 phenotypic ratio, because TT and Tt look identical.</td></tr><tr><td>IAIB is the classic codominant genotype, since red cells of an AB person display both the A and the B antigen.</td></tr><tr><td>A group O child (ii) must receive an i allele from each parent, so both parents must carry i.</td></tr><tr><td>Roan and shell-pattern cattle show codominance, because the red and white hairs appear side by side rather than blending into an intermediate colour.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>x Incomplete dominance = blending. x It's not permanent blending - alleles remain distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>x Codominance = incomplete dominance. x In codominance both show fully, not intermediate.</td></tr><tr><td>x Type AB can donate to anyone. x Type AB can ONLY receive, not donate.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Not every trait follows a simple dominant-recessive pattern, and these exceptions are grouped as non-Mendelian inheritance. In incomplete dominance neither allele is completely dominant, so the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype — red RR x white WW snapdragon gives pink RW — and the phenotypic ratio equals the genotypic ratio of 1:2:1. In codominance both alleles are fully and simultaneously expressed in the heterozygote, as with IA and IB in the ABO blood group system and in roan cattle. The ABO system also illustrates multiple allelism, in which three alleles (IA, IB and i) exist in the population although every individual carries only two.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Mendel's laws of inheritance</td><td>Gregor Mendel worked on the garden pea Pisum sativum from 1856 to 1863 with seven pairs of contrasting characters and formulated the two laws of classical inheritance. The law of segregation states that the two alleles of a gene separate during gamete formation so that each gamete receives one allele, which gives an F2 genotypic ratio of 1:2:1 and a phenotypic ratio of 3:1 in a monohybrid cross. The law of independent assortment states that genes on different chromosomes assort independently during meiosis, giving the 9:3:3:1 F2 phenotypic ratio in a dihybrid cross. Punnett squares, ratio identification and test crosses are the calculation skills tested in NEB entrance papers.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Incomplete dominance and codominance</td><td>Not every trait follows a simple dominant-recessive pattern, and these exceptions are grouped as non-Mendelian inheritance. In incomplete dominance neither allele is completely dominant, so the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype — red RR x white WW snapdragon gives pink RW — and the phenotypic ratio equals the genotypic ratio of 1:2:1. In codominance both alleles are fully and simultaneously expressed in the heterozygote, as with IA and IB in the ABO blood group system and in roan cattle. The ABO system also illustrates multiple allelism, in which three alleles (IA, IB and i) exist in the population although every individual carries only two.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Heredity And Evolution.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Evolution is change in heritable variation in populations across generations. Natural selection, mutation, genetic drift and gene flow can all change allele frequencies; evolution is not an individual changing during its lifetime.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium is a comparison model that assumes a very large population and no selection, mutation or migration. Real populations may violate assumptions; deviations can motivate investigation but do not identify a cause by themselves.</p><h3>Open research question — Predicting complex traits</h3><p>Many traits reflect interactions among multiple genes and environments. How to make predictions that remain accurate across populations and contexts is still a research challenge; a single-gene explanation should not be assumed without evidence.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Heredity And Evolution”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “Predicting complex traits”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-1-population-evolution">OpenStax Biology: population evolution</a></li><li><a href="https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/educational-resources/fact-sheets/human-genomic-variation">NHGRI: human genomic variation</a></li><li><a href="https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Genetics-vs-Genomics">NHGRI: genetics and genomics</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>